{
  "text": "Imagine you're running a lemonade stand. You want to make money, right?\n\n**CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost)** is like how much money you spend to get one person to buy your lemonade. This includes things like the cost of your sign, the lemons, sugar, and cups you use to make the lemonade that attracts them. If you spend $10 on ingredients and marketing, and you get 5 customers, your CAC is $2 per customer ($10 / 5 customers).\n\n**LTV (Lifetime Value)** is the total amount of money you expect to earn from that one customer over the entire time they buy from you. If a customer buys a lemonade from you every week for a month, and each lemonade costs $1, their LTV would be $4 ($1 x 4 weeks).\n\nIn simple terms, you want your LTV to be much higher than your CAC. It means you're making more money from each customer than it costs you to get them, which is essential for a successful business!",
